A||||12||mrfiero||flywheel question||||1||
Z||000000||mrfiero||06-24-2001||10:42 PM||||I should know the answer to this, but I want to double check.<P>I am looking at buying a new flywheel for my '88 GT when I do the clutch.  I know that late 80's/early 90's 2.8/3.1 and the 3.4 DOHC motors are neutrally balanced and share the same flywheel.  Will this flywheel work on my '88?  The GM part # is 24576876.  I believe the only difference between this flywheel and the original '88 is its weight and price (MUCH cheaper than the original).<P>LMK......Thanks.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||tgowens||06-25-2001||01:51 AM||||Mike, seems like nobody knows!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>It would seem logical since they are all internally balanced......and, lighter shouldn't be a problem either, I have seen aluminum flywheels which weigh only 7 lbs. as compared to the 15 lb. stock flywheel installed in Fieros.....now, price? sheesh........lower price is always better when ya can find it, right?<p>[This message has been edited by tgowens (edited 06-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||06-25-2001||02:07 AM||||That's what I was thinking......I cross referenced that flywheel to the 3.1 and 3.4 DOHC engines and both use it, so it should work with the '88 Fiero (all being neutrally balanced).<P>I'm under the assumption that it weighs more than the stock '88 flywheel, which isn't a big deal to me, but if it's lighter I'll take it!  The best part is the price......$57 through gmpartsdirect.com.....MUCH cheaper than the stock flywheel.  <P>Thanks for your input.  BTW......I will be picking up some parts in the San Fran area and might just plan it around the Fiero show in July.  We'll have to wait and see what happens.<P> <p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 06-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||tgowens||06-25-2001||02:43 AM||||hey, that's great -- stop at the show on July 14th if you can - that's show day! If you need directions we have maps posted on the club web site at  <A HREF="http://www.cfog.org" TARGET=_blank>www.cfog.org</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||06-25-2001||04:08 PM||||A heavier flywheel will hurt your performance. It will help your launch but for acceleration it will cause the engine rev up slower. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Steve Normington||06-25-2001||04:43 PM||||Is the flywheel from a RWD? I got a flywheel from a 93 S-10 2.8 at the junkyard and it is about 3/4" bigger in diameter. If the flywheel is from a FWD car, it should be okay.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Steven||06-25-2001||05:49 PM||||NO! NO! NO! Save your money, that is not a flywheel that you want to put on your Fiero!!! Why you ask? Because it is a flexplate, which means it is used on car with automatic transmissions. FYI: It would work for an '88 Fiero w/MD9 trans.||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfiero||06-25-2001||06:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Steven:<BR><B>NO! NO! NO! Save your money, that is not a flywheel that you want to put on your Fiero!!! Why you ask? Because it is a flexplate, which means it is used on car with automatic transmissions. FYI: It would work for an '88 Fiero w/MD9 trans.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Hmmmmm.......I looked it up for a manual tranny, maybe the website listed the wrong part #?  Does anyone have the part # for a FWD neutrally balanced flywheel? ||||reg||1
Z||000008||rockcrawl||06-25-2001||06:04 PM||||If you got that # from the Fiero Online Service Guide (which is down right now) then it is the wrong #. I ran into that before.||||reg||1
